            <ArticleTitle>Trends in Use of Risk-Reducing Mastectomy in a Context of Celebrity Decisions and Media Coverage: An Observational Study in the United States and Australia.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/1475-6773.12774</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine trends in risk-reducing mastectomy (RRM) and immediate breast reconstruction in New York State and New South Wales, especially before and after Angelina Jolie's announcement of her surgery in 2013.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA SOURCE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Hospital discharge data, 2004-2014.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Trends in procedures were assessed using Poisson regression and the Cochrane-Armitage test.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA EXTRACTION METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Extracted using procedure codes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PRINCIPAL FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There was an increase in RRM procedures after May 2013 in both regions, with a peak during late 2013-early 2014. The average bimonthly RRM procedures were significantly higher after Jolie's announcement than before.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In both countries, we found a substantial increase of RRM after Jolie's announcement, highlighting the impact of celebrity health care coverage on preventive surgical choice and the need for decision aids to assist patients.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© Health Research and Educational Trust.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
